# The generated bindings are always checked out with LF, whatever core.autocrlf says (it is true
# on Grace and Zeph). The generator writes LF, so a checkout then compares byte for byte with a
# regeneration (M9 gate e). The gate also compares git blob ids, so it holds either way.
ui/src/bindings/** text eol=lf
ui/src/checksum-kat.json text eol=lf
# The shipped examples are embedded byte for byte (include_bytes!) and tested equal to what is
# written for the user: never convert their line endings.
src-tauri/examples/*.simpa -text
# tauri build rewrites this file with LF on every build. Checked out with CRLF, the rewrite left the
# tree dirty, and the app.exe built from it named its commit "with uncommitted changes" (a fresh
# clone built by tools/ci/build.ps1, 2026-10-10). With LF in the checkout the rewrite is a no-op.
src-tauri/Cargo.toml text eol=lf
